  "summary": "North Richland Hills, Texas invoiced a Flock watchdog $2.3 million for public records about its ALPR program, part of a broader trend of Texas agencies\u2014including Houston's Harris County Sheriff, Irving, DPS, TxDOT, and TDCJ\u2014charging high fees or withholding records about Flock camera use. The article notes that transparency gains from past records requests prompted an audit and prosecution of a Lufkin officer who misused Flock to stalk, and that Gov. Abbott has blocked state funds and paused some installations.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In the Fort Worth-area suburb of North Richland Hills, getting answers about how police use their Flock Safety cameras could cost $2.3 million.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "The invoice said it was accounting for labor costs of $15 an hour \u2014 equivalent to more than 14 years of work \u2014 to search about a terabyte worth of communications about errors, misuse and effectiveness of the Flock system.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    },
    {
      "quote": "When asked for a third set of search data shortly after, DPS withheld the records completely and challenged the request, asking the Attorney General's Office to allow it to withhold the entirety of the search data it previously provided.",
      "paragraph_idx": 39
    },
    {
      "quote": "Out of the hundreds of agencies that use Flock cameras in Texas, only 60 make use of the transparency portals, the Tribune found, representing more than 2,600 of the estimated 13,000 cameras operating in the state. Of those, only 16 share their search records.",
      "paragraph_idx": 48
    }
  ],
